## What a `meta` memory is

**Meta is an abstract supertype** — memorials *about the memory system itself*.  They describe categories, schemas, structural conventions, and operational primitives that the rest of the corpus consumes.  Concrete subtypes carry schema self-description (`type`), typed-edge vocabulary (`predicate`), low-ceremony fleeting capture (`inbox`), durable work queue (`task`), causal-trigger records (`event`), reasoning engines (`engine`), collaborators (`actor` and its subtypes), session / project contexts (`context`), behavior rules (`rule`), and meta-meta type modeling (`meta-type` and subtypes).

Contrast with the sibling abstract supertypes:

- **`artifact`** — produces durable output (decision / plan / library study)
- **`signal`** — captures observation ("here is what we noticed")
- **`guidance`** — prescribes action ("when X happens, do / don't do Y")

Where the other three supertypes deal with the *content* of the corpus (its assertions, observations, prescriptions), `meta` deals with the *structure* of the corpus (what kinds of things exist, how they connect, who created them, when they fired, what rules govern them).

## When `extends: meta` helps

Category-level rules become expressible across the family:

- *"Meta memorials are schema-facing — validation rules apply differently"* — e.g., `inbox` is exempt from reciprocal / body-link audits; `type` self-descriptions don't need empirical grounding
- *"Meta memorials don't carry `supersedes:` the way artifacts do"* — meta is living structure, not versioned output (with exceptions for `predicate` deprecation)
- *"Actor + Engine + Rule + Context + Event compose into the agent-discipline substrate"* — the layer-3 mechanization of behavior compliance

## The Meta sub-tree

The Meta supertype organizes a richer sub-hierarchy than its siblings:

| Subclass             | Role                                              |
|----------------------|---------------------------------------------------|
| `type`               | schema self-description (every type carries a `types/<name>.md`) |
| `predicate`          | typed-edge vocabulary (every predicate carries a `predicates/<name>.md`) |
| `inbox`              | fleeting capture staging                          |
| `task`               | durable actionable work queue                     |
| `event`              | causal-trigger record (something happened at time T) |
| `engine`             | reasoning / projection engine (e.g., codec, rule-engine, search engine) |
| `context`            | session / project / arc context                   |
| `rule`               | behavior rule (LHS pattern → RHS action)          |
| `organization`       | collaborator organization                         |
| `actor` (abstract)   | collaborator (ai / human / system)                |
| └ `ai-actor`         | AI collaborator (model + provider)                |
| └ `human-actor`      | human collaborator                                |
| └ `system-actor`     | system / automation collaborator                  |
| `meta-type` (abstract) | meta-meta type modeling                          |
| └ `markdown-meta-type` | markdown-shaped meta-type                       |
| └ `code-meta-type`   | code-shaped meta-type                             |

The Actor + Context + Rule + Engine + Event quintet forms the substrate for the agent-discipline layer: actors operate in contexts; events fire; engines fire rules in response; rules prescribe action.

## Not instantiated directly

Don't write a memorial with `type: meta` — write one of the concrete subtypes (or, for the sub-abstracts `actor` / `meta-type`, write their further concrete subtypes).  The `abstract?: true` schema flag marks this class as non-instantiable.

